<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Bruce Lee, a 32-year-old man with chronic low back pain since a lifting injury three years ago and a single seizure six weeks ago, came in with a flare of back pain. Two days ago, while practicing a high kick that he had done many times, he twisted and felt the left side of his lower back seize. The pain is a deep ache with spasms across the left low back, 7 out of 10 at worst, worse bending and getting out of a chair and better lying on his side with a hot towel. It does not travel down either leg. He has no numbness or tingling in the legs, the groin or around the back passage, no weakness, and no change in passing urine or stool; he was asked about these specifically because of his old sacral nerve injury. No fever or weight loss since the visit last week. He has taken acetaminophen 1,000 mg three times a day with little relief. He asked for something stronger and asked whether he could have the muscle relaxant a fellow performer uses. He has had no further seizures, has not driven and has used no cannabis. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Two days ago, twisting during a training kick</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Left lower back; no radiation</td></tr><tr><td>Severity</td><td>7 out of 10 at worst</td></tr><tr><td>Quality</td><td>Deep ache with spasms</td></tr><tr><td>Modifying factors</td><td>Worse bending and rising; better lying on his side with heat</td></tr><tr><td>Associated signs and symptoms</td><td>No leg numbness or weakness, no saddle numbness, no bladder or bowel change, no fever</td></tr><tr><td>Context</td><td>Chronic back pain since a sacral nerve injury three years ago; first seizure six weeks ago, no medicine</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

