    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute flare of low back pain with muscle spasm (M62.830)</content>. A mechanical muscle strain on top of his chronic back pain, with no red flags: no saddle numbness, bladder or bowel change, weakness, fever or trauma. Imaging is not needed. Medicine choices are shaped by his recent seizure: tramadol lowers the seizure threshold; cyclobenzaprine and other muscle relaxants sedate, and carisoprodol, which the body turns into meprobamate, can cause withdrawal seizures, so none is prescribed. A short course of an anti-inflammatory is the best-supported medicine for acute low back pain. [S]<list><item>Naproxen 500 mg twice a day with food for seven days; stop acetaminophen while taking it, then use it again as needed.</item><item>Drink normally to thirst while taking naproxen; do not force large volumes of water, since anti-inflammatory medicines reduce the kidneys&apos; ability to clear water.</item><item>Heat, gentle walking and staying active; no kicks, jumps or heavy lifting for a week, then back to the core program with the physical therapist.</item><item>Return at once for numbness in the groin or legs, leg weakness, difficulty passing urine or loss of bowel control.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">First unprovoked seizure (R56.9)</content>. No further events; pain and poor sleep from pain are triggers to watch. [S]<list><item>Keep to the sleep plan; no driving.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Evidence-based guideline: Management of an unprovoked first seizure in adults: Report of the Guideline Development Subcommittee of the American Academy of Neurology and the American Epilepsy Society (2015); Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Preventive visit next week, when the back will be reviewed. Sooner for any warning sign above or any seizure. Next scheduled visit 2026-06-29: periodic adult preventive visit. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
